Abstract = {Today, design is commonly regarded as an essential input for the shaping
   of our physical environment.
   There is an increasing public demand to make design more responsive to
   people, to support design in providing the basis for harmonising any
   physical arrangement to human purposes. Perceptions of the environment
   involve a complex interaction of both physical and social factors, so
   studies of meaning seem crucial to further understanding the complex
   human-environment relationship. In order to assess perceptions of design
   quality, the case of classroom design was used as an example for data
   collection and analysis. First, an exploratory survey was undertaken to
   identify the most important quality characteristics of a classroom, from
   the users' point of views, staff and students. Interviews were then
   carried out using the repertory grid technique, a psychological research
   method based on Kelly's Personal Construct Theory, to elicit users'
   quality perceptions of classroom learning environments. To identify
   common dimensions of perception and experience across groups of
   subjects, a statistical procedure named Generalised Procrustes Analysis
   (GPA) was applied to all the individual grids. Repertory grid technique
   allied with GPA is proposed as a flexible tool for the study of proposed
   architectural environments.},
