Note = {International Symposium on Sustainable Use and Management of Soils in
   Arid and Semiarid Regions, Tech Univ, Muralla del Mar Campus, Cartagena,
   SPAIN, SEP 22-26, 2002},
Abstract = {Soil sealing has become one of the major causes of soil loss and
   degradation in Europe, although it has been less studied and quantified.
   It is also seen as an indicator to assess degree of pressure and
   environmental quality.
   In this work, a methodological structure to evaluate human induced soil
   sealing based on a vector Geographical Information Systems framework is
   developed. Detailed features of urban areas, surfaces covered by
   buildings and communication networks have been digitised using both
   aerial photographs and digital topographic maps at 1:10,000 scale. From
   this basic information soil sealing rates of the last 12 years have been
   obtained.
   Preliminary results from the 1991 aerial photographs of the Castellon
   Province, Spain showed that the concentration of both buildings and
   communication networks increased with decreasing altitude. Although
   sealing seems to be less intense in the province as a whole (2.7\%), up
   to 8.5\% of the soil is sealed by urbanization especially in the
   alluvial plain, transportation infrastructures sealed a Substantial
   amount of land (4.5\%), and buildings sealed somewhat less than the
   transportation infrastructure (4.0\%) in the coastal planes (the land
   below the 100 m contour line) as well.},
