   Ghana},
Booktitle = {HERITAGE 2010: HERITAGE AND SUSTAINABLE DEVELOPMENT, VOLS 1 AND 2},
Year = {2010},
Pages = {683-689},
Note = {2nd International Conference on Heritage and Sustainable Development,
   Evora, PORTUGAL, JUN 22-26, 2010},
Organization = {Turismo Portugal; Full-Service Co; Viagens Abreu},
Abstract = {This study examines the characteristics of heritage tourism development
   in Danish-Osu, Ghana, with emphasis on the preferred scale of
   development, and discusses how residents' perceptions affect sustainable
   urban development. The methodology used to gather primary data between
   October and December 2007 on the preferred scale of heritage tourism
   development included questionnaire survey of household heads, in-depth
   interviews with traditional authorities and opinion leaders, and focus
   group discussions with the youth and market women. The results show that
   due to the characteristics of heritage tourism development in the
   community, residents favour large-scale tourism development, though in a
   better managed way. Residents perceived both positive and negative
   impacts of tourism development-socio-economic benefits being considered
   the most significant indicator of large-scale development. The study
   then discusses the shortcomings of this short-term approach and suggests
   that this short term view of `gain' might be overcome through a greater
   understanding of how residents interact with the urban environment and
   its heritage resources.},
